WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.070
A focus in Tasmania is to look at how

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.640
the devil may save itself through
evolution and

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.800
to accelerate that process. 

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.400
Our research is showing that the devil is changing. 

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:20.500
In the 10 years since disease we have seen a really big reduction in aggressive behaviour in devils. 

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.900
Aggressive behaviour is associated with spreading the disease.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.500
I think the best scenario for Tasmanian devils to be safe from extinction

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.100
is a combination of factors

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:35.800
and I don't think there's one solution or a silver bullet to avoid extinction

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.500
and that combination of factors are devil factors and tumour factors.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.200
From the devil perspective they have to
learn, or be selected, to be less aggressive

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.230
to bite each other less and less often so
they're less likely to pass the disease

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:52.020
and from the tumour perspective also, the
tumour has to evolve into a tumour that

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.000
allows the Tasmanian devil to live long enough to create a next-generation,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.400
 to breed and put a new generation into the
population.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.700
So I think there are parallel things happening together

00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:03.899
that will decrease the likelihood of
extinction.

